<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  org.nextbsd.getty.console.plist

  Console getty service. Shape is modelled on Apple's
  /System/Library/LaunchDaemons/com.apple.getty.plist on macOS:

      ProgramArguments = ("/usr/libexec/getty", "std.9600", "console")
      KeepAlive = true
      RunAtLoad = true (implicit in Apple's com.apple.getty; we set it
                       explicitly so first-boot fires getty without
                       waiting on an external trigger)

  Why org.nextbsd.getty and not com.apple.getty:
    The com.apple.* namespace is for services that mirror what Apple
    actually ships. This one no longer does. Apple's com.apple.getty is
    Disabled=true out of the box and its /etc/ttys hands `console` to
    loginwindow — a stock Mac runs NO getty at all, on any tty; getty is
    the opt-in fallback for console-mode boots. NextBSD has no
    loginwindow, so it ships this job ENABLED, with FreeBSD's
    /usr/libexec/getty and a FreeBSD gettytab class (3wire.9600, see
    below), plus a companion framebuffer job Apple has no equivalent of
    (org.nextbsd.getty.ttyv0). Behaviour, binary, class and topology all
    diverge, so the label says NextBSD. Matches the precedent set by
    org.nextbsd.wland.
    Note for upgrades: the plists are package-owned, so pkg replaces the
    old com.apple.getty.plist. A tree that somehow keeps both just ends
    up with two gettys on one tty, which is harmless — the loser exits 0
    immediately (measured; see org.nextbsd.getty.ttyv0.plist).

  Why /dev/console (not ttyu0 / ttyv0):
    On both Darwin and FreeBSD, /dev/console is a kernel-provided
    character device routed to whichever console driver is active for
    this boot — UART on serial-only boots, vt(4) on VGA. The launchd
    job doesn't have to know which physical device is behind it; the
    kernel handles the multiplexing. This eliminates the missing-device
    respawn-loop problem and removes the need for the per-device
    wrapper script that the earlier two-plist approach used.

    CAVEAT (this is why org.nextbsd.getty.ttyv0.plist exists): on a
    dual-console boot the multiplexing covers kernel MESSAGES only.
    printf -> cnputc walks cn_devlist and writes to every attached
    console, but the console TTY is singular — cninit() ends with
    cnselect(best_cn), and cnadd()/cnselect() call ttyconsdev_select(),
    binding /dev/console to exactly ONE underlying tty. So with
    boot_multicons="YES" you get messages on both consoles and this
    getty on one of them. On arm64 that one is always the UART, because
    the EFI loader publishes hw.uart.console from ACPI SPCR unbidden —
    leaving a machine with no serial cable attached with no reachable
    login at all. See org.nextbsd.getty.ttyv0.plist for the full analysis
    and for why running both jobs is safe where they coincide.

  A login on a serial port that is NOT the console:
    Deliberately not shipped. Where a serial port IS the console this
    job already serves it, so nothing is missing; what is absent is a
    login on a SECOND UART, or serial access on a machine whose console
    is the screen. Both ancestors refuse that by default — FreeBSD's
    ttyu0-3 are `onifconsole` (a login only when that port is already
    the console) and Apple's tty.serial is `off` — because a serial port
    is a wire to an unknown peer: a modem, a UPS, a console server, or a
    device that streams bytes forever. A getty there is an
    unauthenticated root login on a port that may not be in the same
    building, and on a chattering line it churns on the noise.

    An admin who wants it drops in a job of their own:

        Label            org.nextbsd.getty.ttyu1
        ProgramArguments /bin/sh -c
                         'test -c /dev/ttyu1 || exit 0;
                          exec /usr/libexec/getty 3wire.9600 ttyu1'
        RunAtLoad        true
        KeepAlive        true

    The `test -c` guard is not optional: pointed at an absent device,
    getty fails, exits, and KeepAlive respawns it every ThrottleInterval
    forever (measured: 3 log lines per 30s, permanently).

    NOTE: such a job cannot be shipped here pre-disabled. launchd honours
    Disabled=true in `launchctl load` ("nothing found to load") but its
    BOOT-TIME plist scan ignores the key — verified on the arm64 image: a
    Disabled=true job was in `launchctl list` and had run after a reboot.
    So a shipped-but-off entry, the shape FreeBSD and Apple both use,
    is not expressible until that is fixed.

  Why 3wire.9600 (gettytab class), not Apple's std.9600 (#355):
    Apple's plist says std.9600, but FreeBSD's std.* classes lack the
    nc (no-carrier) capability, so getty never sets CLOCAL on the
    console tty. When /dev/console is a serial UART without DCD
    (qemu's pl011 on arm64/virt never asserts it; 3-wire serial on
    real boards doesn't either), the post-login shell then hangs in
    its job-control open("/dev/tty") inside ttydev_open, waiting for
    carrier — banner prints, no prompt ever (nextbsd#355). 3wire.9600
    is the BSD-inherited class for exactly this (nc -> CLOCAL); it is
    what stock FreeBSD /etc/ttys uses for serial consoles. Same baud,
    same vt100 default TERM; a user wanting a richer TERM can
    override after login. On amd64 the bug is masked only because
    qemu's 16550 always reports DCD.

  We keep using FreeBSD's /usr/libexec/getty for now (BSD-derived,
  works on /dev/console identically to Apple's getty). Porting
  Apple's getty itself is deferred until after the broader pkgbase
  cleanup pass per the user roadmap.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>org.nextbsd.getty.console</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/usr/libexec/getty</string>
        <string>3wire.9600</string>
        <string>console</string>
    </array>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
